1 NAME Rita Ann /Stewart/
2 GIVN Rita Ann
2 SURN Stewart
2 _MARNM Bidlake-Anderson
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Mountainview Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Billings
3 STAE MT
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 5 NOV 1933
2 ADDR
3 CITY Billings
3 STAE MT
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 JUL 2010
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Obituary: Beloved wife, mother, grandmother, friend, matriarch passed peacefully with her son John and daughter-in-law Rhonda by her side. Rita was born in Billings to parents Veronica “Peg” Stewart and Colin Stewa
2 CONC rt, Nov. 5, 1933. Rita was educated in Billings schools and attended Eastern Montana College, where she earned her associate’s degree in art. During her time in college, on a bet, she met John “Jack” Morgan Bidlake. It was love at
2 CONC  first sight! John “Jack” Morgan Bidlake and Rita Ann Stewart were married in Livingston on Oct. 5, 1957.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple was blessed with two sons, John Jeffry and Douglas Lee. In March 1974, Jack passed at age 39, due to complications of diabetes. As a young widow, Rita had the sole responsibility of raising two young boys. She also was 
2 CONC challenged with the requirement to obtain her insurance license to keep Bidlake Agency in operation. She did just that, owning and operating the agency for nearly 15 years. Rita was always well organized, and was meticulous about 
2 CONC record keeping all of her life. Rita earned the respect of her peers and served as president of the Independent Insurance Agents Association of the Midland Empire. In 1975, Rita met and married her other love, Jerome “Jerry” Ander
2 CONC son. Jerry’s career as an attorney and lobbyist led them to Helena during legislative sessions and, in 1988, they moved there and Rita officially retired.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rita was a gourmet cook and loved creating meals for family and her many friends. She was famous for her wild game dinners and, on certain occasions, fed up to 250 people in their home. Rita and Jerry loved to travel. On one parti
2 CONC cular trip, she fell ill and was restricted to staying inside. She took advantage of the opportunity by handwriting two cookbooks containing traditional family recipes, as well as some of her personal favorites that she acquired f
2 CONC rom friends and professional chefs that she was able to convince to share the recipes with her. In the book, she made personal notes regarding each recipe and, if it was not her personal creation, gave credit to the friend or chef
2 CONC  from whom she had acquired the recipe, creating not just a cookbook, but a treasured family heirloom. We can still hear her say, ‘I got a good do on that one”! Holidays and birthdays were important to Rita and were celebrated eve
2 CONC nts, especially Christmas. During Rita and Jerry’s travels, Mom would seek out a Christmas store and buy a tree ornament that would be reminiscent of that special time or place. Rita was also known for her flamboyance in style tre
2 CONC nds, from her hair, jewelry, clothing and even eyeglass frames. Rita was a true animal lover and cherished her time with her three cats, Tangueray, Bombay and Gordon, as well as Mr. BIG the puppy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rita returned to Billings in March of 2009 after her husband’s passing. Although Rita’s health began to fail, she always loved time spent with family and her many friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rita was an extraordinary woman; all who knew her would agree. She will always be our Queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rita was preceded in death by her parents, Peg and Colin; brother Tom; husbands Jack Bidlake, Jerome Anderson; son Douglas; infant grandson Zachary John Bidlake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors: John J. Bidlake, son; Rhonda Ann Bidlake, daughter-in-law; Morgan Ann Bidlake, Colin John Bidlake, grandchildren; Chris Anderson, Trisha (Dennis) Halden, stepdaughters; Jack Anderson, stepson; Chelsea Kimpton, Whitney K
2 CONC impton and Tori Kimpton, step-granddaughters; Dana Halden and Christopher Halden, step-grandsons; and Sid Stewart, brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Services will be held at 2 p.m. Monday, Aug. 2, at Smith Downtown Chapel. Reception to follow at the Elk’s Club.
2 CONT
2 CONT In lieu of flowers and in memory of Rita, donations can be sent to the Montana Insurance Education Foundation, 3131 Drudge Drive, Helena, MT 59601.
2 CONT
2 CONT We would especially like to thank the wonderful staff at the Tender Nest and Rocky Mountain Hospice for their support and for taking such good care of Rita during her time there.
2 CONT
