1 NAME Andreas /Gjesdal/
2 GIVN Andreas
2 _MARNM Gjesdal
1 NAME Andreas Fanuelson Gjesdal
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1860
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1920
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fra [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/44804689/andrew-gjesdahl findagrave.com]:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Gjesdahl was the youngest of 12 children in a "well educated family" in Norway, according to Gladyce (Christopherson) Grindahl. She understood members had attended the university and that Andrew was a graduate of it. I did 
2 CONC check at the University of Oslo in 1996. No record of Ańdrew Gjesdal ever attending it. It's possible he attended it very briefly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Gjesdahl and Gunhild Nevland were married Nov. 25, 1881 in Norway. A wedding picture was taken. It is said they traveled to America on their honeymoon. One of their wedding gifts, a beige blanket, still existed recently. Gl
2 CONC adyce Grindahl had half of it. Do not know where it is now.
2 CONT
2 CONT They settled in Minneapolis, Minnesota, where Andrew worked for a milling company. They lived in the Cedar-Franklin area. According to a 1973 Mpls. Star paper on immigrants, the Cedar-Riverside area was a Swedish-Norwegian settlem
2 CONC ent. Pictures were taken at 315 Cedar. Etta Chrìstopherson was unable to find the house in the early 1930's. Accordìng to Mpls. directories, they were frequently on the move. Their first 5 children were born ín Mpls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1891 and 1893 they moved to Cottonwood, MN. Six more children were born. They lived on farms including Dr. Laws farm & Joe Carans farm. The Oftedahls were neighbors and Oftedahls had been friends & neighbors in Norway also
2 CONC . Andrew worked on threshing crews. Etta would travel with her father and the crew as their hired girl and work at the farms they threshed at. Gjesdahls evidently were members of St. Lucas Lutheran Church near Cottonwood. Some of 
2 CONC their children are buried there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1913 they, along with Fred Lende from Granite Falls, moved up to Chester Township near Trail, MN. Andrew Gjesdahls settled on what we knew as the Swanson farm between the Ole Breivold & Selvín Christopherson farms. They built a
2 CONC n addition onto the back of the log house. They sold the farm about 1919 to Swansons, and moved to a small farm of about 18 acres near Crookston, MN. when on the Swanson farm, Andrew sold Mc Connens products. Earlier pictures were
2 CONC  taken of Andrew with his horse, Frank. Andrew & Thormod Fossand (married to sister of Christ Chrístopherson) were good friends. Thormod was from the same area in Norway as Andrew. Andrew was described as a humorous full of fun pe
2 CONC rson, always joking and playing with children. Gunhíld also was a jolly person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew's death in 1920 resulted from a car-train accident near Crookston not far from Marcoux Corner on Thanksgiving Day. His son, Carl, son-in-law, William Riel, and nephew Peter Bolstad were also killed. Peter Bolstad's mother w
2 CONC as a sister to Andrew.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Gjesdahl Riel & son, Curtis, moved ín with her mother. They lived there till Margaret remarried to John Paulson. They then moved to John's house in Crookston. He was an employee of the post office.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunhíld Gjesdahl, along with N.D. Gjesdal folk, made a trip to Norway ín the early 1930's. She visited her mother, brothers, sisters. A picture was taken of the group.
2 CONT Gunhìld lived in Crookston till her death '1n 1934. Mr. & Mrs. Gjesdahl are buried ín Oakdale Cemetery in Crookston, MN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Torger Gjesdahl, brother of Andrew, lived in Tacoma, Washington. He worked for the railroad did cabinet finishing. In his last years of life, he lived with his sister-in-law, Gunhild, in Crookston. Picture of him on porch wi
2 CONC th Gunhild and 2 other ladies (Margaret & a Mrs. Oftedahl). See Christopherson Farm & Etta C. write up for additional mention of Torger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many Norwegíans settled in washington. Ballard ís a Norwegian settlement of Seattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Gjesdahl had a homestead ín Glendive, Montana but let it go in 1919. The Oftedahls lived at Glendive. Mrs. Andrew Gjesdahl and Mrs. Oftedahl (Judìtte) were sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Retyped 2000. Janet Madson
