3 CITY Jamestown
3 STAE ND
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 NOV 1842
2 PLAC Gorjna Lokvica #6
2 ADDR
3 STAE Metlika
3 CTRY Slovenia
1 DEAT
2 DATE 31 JAN 1916
2 ADDR
3 CITY Jamestown
3 STAE ND
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Immigrated in 1868
2 CONT
2 CONT  Metlika, Slovenia and Mottling, Krain, Austria
2 CONT are the same location. 
2 CONT Mottling  is the German name that was used during the Hapsburg monarchy (Austro- Hungarian Empire) and during WWII German occupation. 
2 CONT Laibach is German name of Ljubljana
2 CONT Metlika is the Slavic name- the name currently used. 
2 CONT
2 CONT TUESDAY, FEBRUARY 1, 1916 MARTIN MUTZ PASSED AWAY
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of a well-known Stutsman County Pioneer - 74 years old
2 CONT
2 CONT MARTIN MUTZ, a resident of Stutsman County since 1879 and one of the best known early settlers of this vicinity, passed away at his Jamestown residence, 215 Eighth Avenue, South, Monday evening, death being due to heart trouble an
2 CONC d Bright's Disease with complications. He was 74 years old. He had been ill for two months and was treated at Parkview Hospital for a short time, returning to his home, where he failed rapidly, death coming at 5 o'clock Monday.
2 CONT
2 CONT The passing of Martin Mutz removes a pioneer of this county whose name has long been connected with its growth. Born at Leibach, Austria, he immigrated to America in 1868, coming first to Illinois, then to Wisconsin and settling i
2 CONC n 1879 upon the Mutz homestead near Fried, Stutsman County, nine miles northeast of Jamestown. He was a thrifty, successful farmer, the family passing through the vicissitudes of the early days of North Dakota and gradually acquir
2 CONC ing more land, typifying the success that comes to the ambitious and capable immigrant from Europe in this country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin Mutz was married in 1875 and there are surviving, his widow, Theresia Mutz and seven children, four sons, Anton, Joseph, Roman and Phillip Mutz of Jamestown and three daughters, Mrs. Anna Weber and Miss Catherin and Margare
2 CONC t Mutz also of this city. There are three grandchildren, Ralph Weber and Gerard and Frances Mutz. A cousin John Mutz farms near the original Mutz homestead. There are two brothers, Marcus Mutz of Shell Lake Wisconsin who visited J
2 CONC amestown a few weeks ago, and John Mutz, still at the old home in Leibach, Austria.
